Fixed and committed as e285c907: passing a key with --api-key now skips the first-run setup wizard. Build, tests, clippy and fmt all pass.

What was wrong: in a fresh HOME with no *_API_KEY, yoyo --provider openai --model gpt-4o --api-key dummy opened "Step 1: Choose your AI provider", and pressing Enter fell back to Anthropic. The wizard decision only checked environment variables and config files, never the command line.

What changed:
• The decision: setup::needs_setup_from(provider, cli, env, config) now has a third input. A key from any of the three skips the wizard.
• Where the input comes from: cli::parse_model_config already decides whether to use the --api-key value. That same decision is now carried out as Config::api_key_from_cli and passed to the wizard check in main.rs. I didn't read it from the set_key_source global, which is switched off under cfg!(test), so this is tested in-process.
• Empty key: --api-key "" and --api-key "   " never reach the wizard. The existing flag check refuses them with exit 1 first. I pinned that in a test.
• Tests: they live in a new file, src/setup_needs_tests.rs, because setup.rs has no room left under its size limit. The old decision table moved there and gained the --api-key column. I checked the table can catch the bug: removing the cli input made it fail on the --api-key-only row, and restoring it made it pass. A second test drives the real argument parser.
• Size limits: moving the table shrank setup.rs from 2101 to 2054 lines; cli.rs grew 7769 → 7774. I updated both entries in tests/module_size.rs.

Checked on the real binary: I ran it in a pseudo-terminal with script, a fresh HOME and env -i. With --api-key dummy it goes straight to the REPL (provider: openai, model: gpt-4o). Without the flag it still opens "Step 1: Choose your AI provider". Both outputs are pasted in the commit body.

Docs and pointers: quick-start.md and models.md now say --api-key skips the wizard. The ARCHITECTURE.md line saying the wizard door was "still open on #1062" is marked superseded. None of the #1062 mentions in src, docs or skills tells a reader the ticket is open, so the auto-close from the "Fixes" title won't leave a dead pointer.
